Date: Mon, 18 Jan 93 19:38:01 CST
From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
Subject: PPL: Why Not Let Convicts Run the Police?

Robin Hanson posts:

>I don't think it is obvious that we should each prefer a law which
>publicizes every verdict.  This information is valuable to the rest of
>society, but is the sort of public good which is likely to be
>underproduced in a PPL.  

Agreed.  But I don't imagine criminal records would be public in any case. 
Rather, I think they would probably be treated much the way credit records
are now: privately held and available only at a price.  I call it "legal
credit," and people who run low on it in a PPL face partial or total
ostracism.

>Looking after my self interest, I might rather
>negotiate for a higher restitution, should I be a victim, than
>insisting that the verdict be publicized.  

I think, instead, that a victim would have to *pay* to earn the exclusive
right to the information generated by the operations of a private legal
system that catches and convicts a criminal.  Remember, legal credit is a
form of intellectual property that others will find very useful.  It will
therefore probably be very valuable.  Sure, potential victims can enter
into protection contracts that give them the rights to the criminal records
of their aggressors, but such contracts will cost more than alternative
contracts that vest those valuable rights in the protection agency. 
Protection agencies will quite rightly view the generation of legal credit
ratings as an additional source of income.

>Similarly, as a potential
>criminal, I might rather agree to pay a higher crime that to get my
>name trashed over any little offense.

Now, this seems to get the incentives right--the criminal would have to pay
to keep his or her record clean.  But note that reputational interests of
those who compile and sell "legal credit" ratings may preclude them from
offering exemption at moderate prices.

>>Ostracism may be the worst of all the calamities to befall a caught
>>criminal.
>
>Which is why they might not agree to it.

Maybe not, but the point to keep in mind is that it is a heavy cost to
which caught criminals are easily susceptible.  It will often be much
easier to sell someone information about a criminal's past actions than it
will be to actually catch that criminal and force her to pay restitution. 
Whether or not criminals like ostracism, it must be taken into account when
when try to estimate what sort of bargaining power they will have to set
the terms of their punishments.  The ostracism option leaves them
comparatively worse off.


