Date:    Mon, 18 Jan 93 23:20 EST
From: "Robert H Michels"                          <MICHELS@UNC.OIT.UNC.EDU>
Subject: Why hair does matter.

In my previous message I wrote that we do care about what otherpeople
think about us, especially at first.  Perry asks what this has to do
with extropianism, and I think it has a lot to do with it.  Tom writes
that he brought the issue up only for the sake of humor, but there's
more to it.  We are interested in promoting certain memes which we think
will better the circumstances in which we live.  Certain behaviors are
more likely to make this an easier task than others.  Giving others good
first impressions is a useful (if not down right enjoyable) way of
promoting the spread of good memes.  Good manners, intelligent
conversation, and certain kinds of grooming make for good first
impressions.  Haircuts tell a lot about a person; specifically they
can convey what the haircut wearer wants others to think.  A conservativ
cut can in general convey an attitude of seriousness, or at least let
others believe that that is what you want conveyed.  Long hair conveys
a rebellious attitude.  Mohawks -:-) and skinheads (:-) convey an
attitude of radicalism or fanaticism.  These are grossly inaccurate
ways of conveying information, but they do influence first impressions
and first impressions matter.
     Now, is there an extropian haircut?  Yes, several in fact.
Whatever lends itself to the spread of extropian memes is extropian.
Long hair may help some of us attract other rebels, but at the same
time, a conservative cut may convey the information that we are very
serious about what we value and believe and that we want and expect to
be taken seriously.  If we are slobs, then we attract other slobs and
are not taken seriously.  Of course I exaggerate here, for there have
been some slobs who were taken seriously by tidy people. But given that
we can make use of something that is so low cost as haircuts and
grooming with the possibility of influencing who we meet and what their
first impressions are, we ought to utilize it.  If you really do not
care what people think of your appearance, you might want to reconsider
what you care about.
Rob Michels
michels@unc.bitnet

