From: Marc.Ringuette@GS80.SP.CS.CMU.EDU
Subject: Re: PPL: Legal Credit Ratings

Robin> If I sign up for a law which allows verdicts to be hidden,
Robin> then people who judge my credit record will have to take that 
Robin> into account, knowing that it might not show the whole story.  

Or else the people who judge your credit record will choose to buy
better information.  I don't think it's going to be practically
possible for a criminal to pay extra to suppress publication of a
crime.  Any of a number of employees of the several agencies involved
has the opportunity to sell his name to a "criminal clearinghouse".
This clearinghouse makes a business of selling complete-as-possible
information on criminal activity, so can afford to pay informants.

There do seem to be two obstacles to the creation of such a clearinghouse,
however.  First, agencies could ferret out informants by carefully
controlling who knows about a given incident, and matching employees with
known leaks.  Second, the clearinghouse would be vulnerable to fraud by
informants who may invent fictitious information to sell.


-- Marc Ringuette (mnr@cs.cmu.edu)

